==== TEST 38387: hardware ACL (permit + deny) on a BACKUP member port ====
started 2026-08-18T09:58:41+12:00
bench   : tb470, AT-IE520-28GSX stack, chassis-id 3439, VMAC 0000.cd37.0d6f
          software IE520-tb470.rel tomahawk_ie520-continuous, Build Fri Aug 14 2026
          member 1 = 264A23066 / 84e3.2787.0740 / console /dev/u5
          member 2 = 264A23052 / 84e3.2787.09c0 / console /dev/u4
target  : BACKUP = stack member 1, ingress port port1.0.1
path    : tb470 eth3  ->  port1.0.1   (direct, isolated from the shared lab segment)
dest    : 10.38.215.66  (stack VLAN interface)
sources : 10.38.215.65  = expected PERMIT (explicit permit rule)
          10.38.215.67 = expected DENY   (explicit deny rule)
KEY SEMANTIC (from ck.db CLI ref, ipv4_hw_acl_cmd/accesslist_hardware_named_bi.html):
  Hardware ACLs will permit access unless explicitly denied by an ACL action.
  => the implicit action is PERMIT, not deny. A permit rule alone proves nothing, so
     this test measures the DENY rule and requires COUNTER HITS on BOTH rules as
     positive evidence rather than relying on 'traffic got through'.
  the ACL will only apply to incoming data packets  => ingress only.
NOTE: software ACLs cannot be applied to a port on this platform (access-group offers
      hardware lists only; 'ip access-group' is unrecognised on port and VLAN
      interfaces; 'access-class' unrecognised on line vty). Hardware ACL is the only
      option, consistent with the test title.

######## PRE-ACL CONTROL — all four source/dest pairs reachable, NO ACL applied ########
tb470 secondary source addresses added: eth2 +10.38.215.35/27, eth3 +10.38.215.67/27
  10.38.215.33 -> 10.38.215.34   MASTER permit-src    0% packet loss
  10.38.215.35 -> 10.38.215.34   MASTER deny-src      0% packet loss
  10.38.215.65 -> 10.38.215.66   BACKUP permit-src    0% packet loss
  10.38.215.67 -> 10.38.215.66   BACKUP deny-src      0% packet loss
=> Every path works BEFORE any ACL exists. Any later failure is therefore attributable
   to the ACL and not to an unreachable source, a missing route or a dead path.

######## STEP 1 RESULT — ACL configured and applied ########
access-list hardware acl-backup
    4 permit icmp 10.38.215.65/32 10.38.215.66/32
    8 deny   icmp 10.38.215.67/32 10.38.215.66/32
interface port1.0.1  ->  access-group acl-backup      [stack member 1 = BACKUP]
(sequence numbers auto-assigned as multiples of 4, per the CLI reference)

######## STEP 2 RESULT — traffic follows the ACL rules ########
                                              expected   observed
  10.38.215.65 -> 10.38.215.66   (matches PERMIT)    pass       0% packet loss
  10.38.215.67 -> 10.38.215.66   (matches DENY)      blocked  100% packet loss
Compare against the PRE-ACL CONTROL above, where BOTH sources reached the target at
0% loss. The only change is the ACL, so the 100% loss is the deny rule acting.

POSITIVE EVIDENCE — show access-list counters (4 echo requests sent per source):
  Name: acl-backup
      Rule: permit icmp 10.38.215.65/32 10.38.215.66/32
      Packet Hits: 4
      Rule: deny icmp 10.38.215.67/32 10.38.215.66/32
      Packet Hits: 4
Both rules register EXACTLY the 4 packets sent. This matters because hardware ACLs
implicitly PERMIT: without a counter hit, "traffic got through" would be equally
consistent with the permit rule never matching at all. The hit count rules that out.

######## VERDICT: PASS ########
Traffic followed the ACL rules on the BACKUP member's port. Both the explicit permit
and the explicit deny behaved as specified, each confirmed by rule counters.

CROSS-REFERENCE: run jointly with TEST 38389 (MASTER member port) in the same
session, same stack, same software, same procedure. Both members produced IDENTICAL
results - hardware ACL enforcement shows no master/backup difference.
